1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,379
Sam didn't script one ad and call the section done.

2
00:00:03,379 --> 00:00:08,189
He ran three angles through this exact five-step pipeline in one sitting, because

3
00:00:08,189 --> 00:00:12,821
Section 1.4 already proved one video tells you nothing about which angle wins.

4
00:00:12,821 --> 00:00:15,731
Now it's your turn, same steps, your own product.

5
00:00:15,731 --> 00:00:20,541
Pull up the angle map from Section 2.4 and the script structure from Section 4.2.

6
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:25,470
Pick three angles you haven't scripted yet, different enough from each other that a

7
00:00:25,470 --> 00:00:28,558
test between them would actually tell you something.

8
00:00:28,558 --> 00:00:33,369
Then run each one through the full pipeline this section just taught you: script,

9
00:00:33,369 --> 00:00:35,210
avatar, product shot, assembly.

10
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:38,713
Why three specifically, and not five, or one done properly?

11
00:00:38,713 --> 00:00:43,167
Five is more than a first workshop needs, and one tells you nothing at all.

12
00:00:43,167 --> 00:00:47,978
Three is the smallest batch that actually behaves like a test instead of a guess.

13
00:00:47,978 --> 00:00:52,728
This is also the point where video stops being separate lectures and becomes one

14
00:00:52,728 --> 00:00:57,539
continuous piece of work, the way it will every week once you're running this for

15
00:00:57,539 --> 00:00:57,836
real.

16
00:00:57,836 --> 00:01:02,527
Nobody scripts a single ad, waits, generates an avatar clip in isolation, waits

17
00:01:02,527 --> 00:01:04,665
again, then assembles it days later.

18
00:01:04,665 --> 00:01:09,416
You'll move through all five steps for each of your three angles in one sitting,

19
00:01:09,416 --> 00:01:12,445
which is exactly the rhythm the actual job runs on.

20
00:01:12,995 --> 00:01:18,250
This is the actual template, and every line traces back to a lecture you've already

21
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:19,832
sat through this section.

22
00:01:19,832 --> 00:01:25,087
The format decision is Section 4.2's word budget, applied before a single word gets

23
00:01:25,087 --> 00:01:25,594
written.

24
00:01:25,594 --> 00:01:30,722
The script step is that same lecture's structure: hook, proof, CTA, nothing else,

25
00:01:30,722 --> 00:01:32,432
checked aloud with a timer.

26
00:01:32,432 --> 00:01:37,370
The avatar step pulls straight from Section 4.3: licence tier confirmed first,

27
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:42,245
delivery style chosen to match the hook rather than a generic upbeat default.

28
00:01:42,245 --> 00:01:47,500
The product shot is Section 4.4's honest split, one real clip, filmed once, reused.

29
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:52,628
And assembly is last lecture's discipline: captions exact, timed right, the whole

30
00:01:52,628 --> 00:01:55,351
thing tested muted before you call it done.

31
00:01:55,351 --> 00:02:00,479
Fill this out for each of your three angles and you're not improvising a video ad

32
00:02:00,479 --> 00:02:01,302
from scratch.

33
00:02:01,302 --> 00:02:06,621
You're answering five questions you already know the right answers to, in order, the

34
00:02:06,621 --> 00:02:11,749
same way the static workshop turned Section 3 into a checklist instead of a blank

35
00:02:11,749 --> 00:02:12,066
page.

36
00:02:12,066 --> 00:02:17,320
Notice what this template deliberately leaves out: nothing here asks whether the ad

37
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:17,827
is good.

38
00:02:17,827 --> 00:02:20,043
That question belongs to Section 8.

39
00:02:20,043 --> 00:02:25,235
This template only asks whether the ad is correct: licensed, accurately captioned,

40
00:02:25,235 --> 00:02:28,210
built to a real word budget, watchable on mute.

41
00:02:28,210 --> 00:02:33,402
Get those five questions right on all three, and you've done everything a workshop

42
00:02:33,402 --> 00:02:35,238
can ask of you before a test.

43
00:02:35,788 --> 00:02:40,741
About an hour and twenty minutes, total, and notice the product filming step only

44
00:02:40,741 --> 00:02:43,982
happens once even though it feeds three separate ads.

45
00:02:43,982 --> 00:02:49,119
That's Section 4.4's reuse argument, showing up here as real time saved instead of a

46
00:02:49,119 --> 00:02:49,975
lecture point.

47
00:02:49,975 --> 00:02:54,501
If one of your three avatar clips comes back flat on the first generation,

48
00:02:54,501 --> 00:02:58,965
regenerate it rather than settling, exactly like Section 4.3 told you to.

49
00:02:58,965 --> 00:03:03,979
A flat delivery on a strong script is a worse ad than either problem alone, and it

50
00:03:03,979 --> 00:03:06,976
costs you nothing but a few minutes to try again.

51
00:03:06,976 --> 00:03:09,544
Notice, too, where the time actually goes.

52
00:03:09,544 --> 00:03:14,436
Scripting and assembly take longer than generation itself, fifty minutes between

53
00:03:14,436 --> 00:03:17,983
them against twenty for the two generation steps combined.

54
00:03:17,983 --> 00:03:20,980
The bottleneck in this workflow was never the AI.

55
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:25,689
It's the same checking and timing work Section 4.2 and 4.5 spent two lectures

56
00:03:25,689 --> 00:03:27,034
teaching you properly.

57
00:03:27,034 --> 00:03:31,376
Treat that split as the honest shape of this job, not a workshop quirk.

58
00:03:31,376 --> 00:03:36,329
Once you're running this weekly, generation will keep getting faster and cheaper.

59
00:03:36,329 --> 00:03:41,405
The scripting and checking time won't shrink nearly as much, because it's you doing

60
00:03:41,405 --> 00:03:42,383
it, not a model.

61
00:03:42,383 --> 00:03:47,153
Budget your own weeks around that reality rather than around whatever a tool's

62
00:03:47,153 --> 00:03:49,355
marketing page promises about speed.

63
00:03:49,905 --> 00:03:54,536
Both of these are the exact mistakes Harlan's first video pass already made, and

64
00:03:54,536 --> 00:03:58,820
they're easy to repeat because neither one shows up as an error on screen.

65
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:03,567
Skip the licence check because a draft looks convincing, and you get Section 4.1's

66
00:04:03,567 --> 00:04:08,257
exact problem: a video that renders perfectly and still isn't cleared to run as a

67
00:04:08,257 --> 00:04:08,720
paid ad.

68
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:13,467
Skip the muted-watch test because it sounds right with your own headphones on, and

69
00:04:13,467 --> 00:04:17,982
you ship an ad built for a condition most of your real audience isn't watching

70
00:04:17,982 --> 00:04:18,330
under.

71
00:04:18,330 --> 00:04:20,240
Neither mistake announces itself.

72
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:24,640
A licence gap doesn't show up in the clip, it shows up in a takedown notice.

73
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:29,156
A sound-dependent cut doesn't fail in your edit software, it fails silently in

74
00:04:29,156 --> 00:04:32,514
someone's feed, on mute, where you'll never see it happen.

75
00:04:32,514 --> 00:04:37,029
That's exactly why both checks are steps in the template rather than things to

76
00:04:37,029 --> 00:04:40,908
remember, so catching them doesn't depend on you catching yourself.

77
00:04:40,908 --> 00:04:45,424
Ask yourself, honestly, which of the two you're more likely to skip under time

78
00:04:45,424 --> 00:04:45,945
pressure.

79
00:04:45,945 --> 00:04:50,576
Most people skip the muted-watch test, because it takes thirty seconds and feels

80
00:04:50,576 --> 00:04:53,007
like the least important step on the list.

81
00:04:53,007 --> 00:04:53,528
It isn't.

82
00:04:53,528 --> 00:04:58,218
It's the step that decides whether most of your audience ever receives the ad you

83
00:04:58,218 --> 00:05:00,591
spent the other seventy minutes building.

84
00:05:01,141 --> 00:05:03,910
One honest expectation before you start cutting.

85
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:08,583
You will end this workshop with an opinion about which of your three ads is best.

86
00:05:08,583 --> 00:05:10,083
Hold that opinion loosely.

87
00:05:10,083 --> 00:05:14,756
Section 0 already showed you, on Harlan's own set, that a founder's favourite and

88
00:05:14,756 --> 00:05:17,525
the actual winner aren't reliably the same clip.

89
00:05:17,525 --> 00:05:20,986
The point of finishing three isn't finding the one you like.

90
00:05:20,986 --> 00:05:25,544
It's building a real spread, different hooks, different proof points, different

91
00:05:25,544 --> 00:05:29,467
avatar deliveries, that a test in Section 7 can actually tell apart.

92
00:05:29,467 --> 00:05:34,313
Measure yourself against that standard when you finish: not "did I make a great ad,"

93
00:05:34,313 --> 00:05:39,043
but "did I make three ads different enough from each other that losing two of them

94
00:05:39,043 --> 00:05:43,831
would actually mean something." If all three feel like minor variations on the same

95
00:05:43,831 --> 00:05:45,850
idea, you haven't built a test yet.

96
00:05:45,850 --> 00:05:47,696
You've built one ad three times.

97
00:05:47,696 --> 00:05:50,062
If that happens, don't reshoot all three.

98
00:05:50,062 --> 00:05:54,735
Go back to the angle map from Section 2.4 and swap out whichever one is doing the

99
00:05:54,735 --> 00:05:59,523
least work, the weakest hook or the most similar proof point, and rebuild just that

100
00:05:59,523 --> 00:05:59,753
one.

101
00:05:59,753 --> 00:06:02,926
The goal was never three perfect ads on the first pass.

102
00:06:02,926 --> 00:06:07,542
It was a spread worth testing, and a spread with one weak link swapped out still

103
00:06:07,542 --> 00:06:07,945
counts.

104
00:06:08,495 --> 00:06:10,419
One lecture left in this section.

105
00:06:10,419 --> 00:06:15,257
Then Section six puts every one of these three ads through the pre-flight checklist

106
00:06:15,257 --> 00:06:19,978
before any of them go near an ad account, and Section seven turns a cleared batch

107
00:06:19,978 --> 00:06:24,233
like this one into an actual test with a budget and a kill rule attached.

108
00:06:24,233 --> 00:06:28,780
Keep whatever you produced here, even the version you're not fully happy with.

109
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:33,035
Nothing in this course asks for perfect creative out of a first workshop.

110
00:06:33,035 --> 00:06:37,581
It asks for three ads, checked correctly, that a real test can tell apart, and

111
00:06:37,581 --> 00:06:41,661
that's exactly what you should have sitting in front of you right now.

112
00:06:41,661 --> 00:06:46,557
Everything from here forward, the pre-flight pass, the testing architecture, the way

113
00:06:46,557 --> 00:06:51,337
you read a result, works on creative you actually made, not a hypothetical example

114
00:06:51,337 --> 00:06:52,095
from a slide.
